  </diagram>
  <diagram id="p19" name="19 · Training Lineage — Base Model → Chat Model">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1400" dy="900" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1720" pageHeight="1770" math="0" shadow="0">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
        <mxCell id="1" parent="0" />
        <mxCell id="p19_t" value="TRAINING LINEAGE — HOW A BASE MODEL BECOMES A HELPFUL CHAT MODEL. One example per stage: PRETRAINING → SFT → RLHF / DPO → DISTILLATION — the pipeline that turns a raw next-word predictor into finzorr&#39;s primary chat model (Groq llama-3.3-70b) and its local emergency-fallback model." style="text;html=1;fontSize=15;fontStyle=1;align=left;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="12" width="1660" height="26" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_intro" value="TRAINING LINEAGE — WHY A BASE MODEL ISN&#39;T YET A CHATBOT&#xa;&#xa;A freshly pretrained (&quot;base&quot;) model only continues text in whatever direction it started — it is not automatically helpful. Turning it into a model that reliably answers questions, follows instructions, and prefers good answers over bad ones takes several further training stages, each adding one specific capability.&#xa;&#xa;This page walks the lineage — PRETRAINING → SFT → RLHF / DPO → DISTILLATION — one small worked example per stage.&#xa;&#xa;finzorr runs NONE of these stages itself: its primary chat model, Groq llama-3.3-70b, arrives at app/infrastructure/llm/ already pretrained, SFT&#39;d, and RLHF/DPO-aligned — finzorr only calls the finished model over an API." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#f5f5f5;strokeColor=#999999;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=6;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="60" width="380" height="270" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_glance" value="THE FIVE STAGES, AT A GLANCE&#xa;&#xa;PRETRAINING — next-token prediction over massive raw text → grammar, facts, general world knowledge.&#xa;&#xa;SFT — instruction/answer PAIRS teach the base model to actually follow instructions and answer, instead of just continuing text.&#xa;&#xa;RLHF — humans rank candidate answers → a reward model learns the ranking → the main model is nudged toward high-reward outputs.&#xa;&#xa;DPO — the same &quot;prefer the better answer&quot; effect as RLHF, adjusted directly from (better, worse) pairs — no separate reward model.&#xa;&#xa;DISTILLATION — a smaller student model imitates a larger teacher model&#39;s outputs, punching above its own parameter count." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#f5f5f5;strokeColor=#999999;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=6;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="350" width="380" height="330" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_corpus" value="Raw text corpus&#xa;billions of examples of ordinary text — web pages, books, articles" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e0f2f1;strokeColor=#00897b;fontSize=11;align=left;spacingLeft=8;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="450" y="60" width="280" height="70" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_s1" value="1 · PRETRAINING&#xa;(the model reads text and learns to predict the next word, over and over)&#xa;e.g. &quot;The capital of France is&quot; → predicts &quot;Paris&quot;, simply because that pattern appeared constantly in training text" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e8f0fe;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;fontSize=11;align=left;spacingLeft=8;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="450" y="140" width="280" height="120" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_o1" value="→ Base / raw model&#xa;continues text; doesn&#39;t yet &quot;answer&quot; on purpose" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ede7f6;strokeColor=#7e57c2;fontSize=11;align=left;spacingLeft=8;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="450" y="270" width="280" height="70" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_s2" value="2 · SFT (Supervised Fine-Tuning)&#xa;(shown instruction→answer PAIRS so it learns to actually answer, not just continue text)&#xa;Before: &quot;What is the capital of France?&quot; → base model often just continues with MORE trivia questions.&#xa;After: same question → answers &quot;Paris.&quot; directly." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e8f0fe;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;fontSize=11;align=left;spacingLeft=8;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="450" y="350" width="280" height="150" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_o2" value="→ Instruction-following model" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ede7f6;strokeColor=#7e57c2;fontSize=11;align=left;spacingLeft=8;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="450" y="510" width="280" height="40" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_s3" value="3 · RLHF&#xa;rank answers → train a reward model → nudge toward high-reward outputs&#xa;e.g. reward(A)≈0.92, reward(B)≈0.10" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;fontSize=11;align=left;spacingLeft=8;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="450" y="560" width="135" height="138" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_s4" value="4 · DPO&#xa;same goal, more direct — straight from (better, worse) pairs, no reward model&#xa;e.g. chosen=A, rejected=B (same S&amp;P pair)" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e8f0fe;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;fontSize=11;align=left;spacingLeft=8;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="595" y="560" width="135" height="138" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_o3" value="→ Aligned chat model" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ede7f6;strokeColor=#7e57c2;fontSize=11;align=left;spacingLeft=8;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="450" y="708" width="280" height="40" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_s5" value="5 · DISTILLATION&#xa;small student model imitates a larger teacher model&#39;s outputs&#xa;e.g. teacher puts most probability on &quot;25&quot;/&quot;50&quot; for a rate-hike prompt" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e8f0fe;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;fontSize=11;align=left;spacingLeft=8;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="450" y="758" width="280" height="100" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_o4" value="→ Distilled student model" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ede7f6;strokeColor=#7e57c2;fontSize=11;align=left;spacingLeft=8;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="450" y="868" width="280" height="40" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_fb" value="FINZORR — LIVE&#xa;local Ollama emergency-fallback model: this lineage is why it still performs above its raw size" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e6f4ea;strokeColor=#34a853;fontSize=11;align=left;spacingLeft=8;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="450" y="918" width="280" height="88" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_reward" value="REWARD MODEL — HOW HUMAN RANKINGS BECOME A SCORE (worked example)&#xa;&#xa;Prompt: &quot;What was the S&amp;P 500&#39;s return in 2023?&quot;&#xa;&#xa;Three candidate answers, ranked BEST → WORST by human raters:&#xa;A (best) — &quot;About 24% in 2023, including dividends.&quot; — accurate and direct.&#xa;C (middle) — &quot;It returned exactly 31.7%.&quot; — confident-sounding, but wrong (hallucinated).&#xa;B (worst) — &quot;It went up a lot, who cares about the exact number?&quot; — dismissive, unhelpful.&#xa;&#xa;A separate, small REWARD MODEL (a model whose only job is to output a score for how good an answer looks) is trained on many such rankings until it can score any new answer, e.g.:&#xa;reward(A) ≈ 0.92     reward(C) ≈ 0.35     reward(B) ≈ 0.10&#xa;&#xa;RLHF then adjusts the main model (via reinforcement learning, e.g. PPO) to raise the probability of producing high-reward answers like A, and lower it for low-reward ones like B and C — including confident-but-wrong answers like C, exactly the failure mode reward models are trained to catch.&#xa;&#xa;RLHF vs DPO — same goal, two routes:&#xa;RLHF: rank answers → train a reward model → run an RL loop against that model&#39;s scores. Two training stages, one extra reward-model artifact to maintain.&#xa;DPO: label the same answers as a (chosen, rejected) pair — chosen = A, rejected = B — and adjust the main model directly on that pair with a single loss function. One training stage, no reward model at all.&#xa;Both nudge the model toward more A-style answers and fewer B/C-style ones; DPO just gets there without the middle step." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e8f0fe;strokeColor=#4285F4;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=6;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="740" y="60" width="440" height="560" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_distill" value="DISTILLATION — TEACHER → STUDENT (worked example)&#xa;&#xa;A smaller STUDENT model is trained to imitate a larger, already-trained TEACHER model&#39;s outputs — specifically the teacher&#39;s full probability spread across plausible next words, not just its single top pick. That richer signal is a &quot;soft label&quot;, and it packs in more of the teacher&#39;s judgment per training example than a single correct-answer label would.&#xa;&#xa;Worked example: for the prompt &quot;The Federal Reserve raised interest rates by 0.&quot;, a large teacher model&#39;s output distribution puts most of its probability on &quot;25&quot; and &quot;50&quot; (real, common central-bank increments in percentage points) and only a sliver on other numbers. A 3B-parameter student trained to match that WHOLE distribution — not just told &quot;the answer is 25&quot; — ends up noticeably more capable than a 3B model trained from scratch on raw text alone, which would have to rediscover which increments are realistic from far less guidance per example.&#xa;&#xa;FINZORR — LIVE: this is the exact lineage behind finzorr&#39;s local emergency-fallback model. When the cloud providers are unavailable, app/infrastructure/llm/&#39;s OpenAICompatibleProvider — the same provider abstraction that calls Groq and Gemini in normal operation — falls back to a small local Ollama model instead. Small, commonly-used local Ollama models are themselves typically distilled from larger teachers rather than trained from scratch, which is why finzorr&#39;s fallback path can still answer usefully during an outage — while remaining, honestly, meaningfully weaker than the primary Groq llama-3.3-70b path, precisely because it is smaller." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#f5f5f5;strokeColor=#999999;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=6;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1200" y="60" width="490" height="560" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_faq" value="QUICK-REFERENCE Q&amp;A&#xa;&#xa;Pretraining vs SFT? Pretraining only teaches next-word prediction over raw text — it has no concept of &quot;answer this question.&quot; SFT is what teaches the pattern &quot;when you see a question, answer it directly,&quot; by training on instruction→answer PAIRS instead of raw continuous text.&#xa;&#xa;RLHF vs DPO — same goal, different route? Both aim to make the model prefer better answers over worse ones. RLHF gets there via an extra trained reward model plus a reinforcement-learning loop; DPO skips the reward model and adjusts the main model directly from (chosen, rejected) answer pairs. Simpler pipeline, similar goal.&#xa;&#xa;Why does distillation help a SMALL model? A student trained to match a teacher&#39;s full output distribution (which numbers/words the teacher considered plausible, and by how much) gets far more signal per training example than a student just told the one &quot;correct&quot; next word — why a distilled 3B model regularly outperforms a 3B model trained from scratch on raw text alone.&#xa;&#xa;Does finzorr train any of this? No. finzorr consumes already-trained models as a service: Groq llama-3.3-70b (primary chat) and, only when cloud providers are unavailable, a small local Ollama model (emergency fallback) — both accessed through the same app/infrastructure/llm/ OpenAICompatibleProvider. finzorr&#39;s own work is prompt-building (app/specialists/nl2sql.py, app/specialists/rag.py), routing (app/orchestration/supervisor.py), and streaming tokens back to the client (app/orchestration/streaming.py) — not training.&#xa;&#xa;Which stage explains hallucination risk? None of them fully eliminates it — next-token prediction is a statistical guess, not a lookup, and RLHF/DPO reduce (see the reward-model example, left) but do not eliminate confidently-wrong answers like candidate C. Grounding (RAG, via app/infrastructure/vector_store.py and app/documents/ingest.py) is what pins answers to real, checkable content." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#f5f5f5;strokeColor=#999999;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=6;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="450" y="1020" width="730" height="440" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_conn" value="WHY THIS MATTERS AT THE APPLICATION LAYER&#xa;&#xa;SFT is why prompt-building works at all — app/specialists/nl2sql.py and app/specialists/rag.py build an instruction-style prompt (a system role plus the user&#39;s question) and simply trust the model to answer it directly rather than continue it with more questions. That trust only holds because the model was SFT&#39;d to follow instructions.&#xa;&#xa;RLHF/DPO alignment is why answers default to polite, direct, and reluctant to confidently invent numbers — see the reward-model example, left — though it reduces, not eliminates, hallucination risk.&#xa;&#xa;Distillation is why the local emergency-fallback model stays useful — it is a distilled small model, not a from-scratch one, called through the same app/infrastructure/llm/ OpenAICompatibleProvider used for Groq and Gemini, just routed to Ollama when the cloud providers are unavailable.&#xa;&#xa;The model itself is stateless between calls — the lineage on this page shapes HOW it responds within one call; conversation memory, routing (app/orchestration/supervisor.py), and token streaming (app/orchestration/streaming.py) are separate, application-layer concerns." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=6;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1200" y="1020" width="490" height="330" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_legend" value="LEGEND (standard color key used across this atlas)&#xa;&#xa;Teal = input / entry — the raw training data a stage starts from&#xa;Blue = process — a training stage that adjusts the model directly (PRETRAINING, SFT, DPO, DISTILLATION), or a reference/worked-example panel&#xa;Yellow = decision point / key computation step — RLHF&#39;s reward-model scoring, and the application-layer takeaways panel&#xa;Purple = output / result — the model&#39;s state after each stage completes&#xa;Green = LIVE — actually built &amp; running in finzorr today&#xa;Gray = dense reference / annotation panel&#xa;&#xa;On THIS page: teal marks the raw text corpus that pretraining starts from; blue/yellow trace the five training-lineage stages in sequence (PRETRAINING → SFT → RLHF/DPO → DISTILLATION); purple marks the resulting model state after each stage; green marks the one LIVE finzorr fact (the local Ollama emergency-fallback model, itself a distilled small model); gray panels hold dense reference and worked-example material. No amber or red is used — this page is training-lineage reference material, not a build-gap or incident page." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#f5f5f5;strokeColor=#999999;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=6;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="1480" width="1650" height="250" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_e1" style="endArrow=block;html=1;strokeColor=#5f6368;" edge="1" parent="1" source="p19_corpus" target="p19_s1">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_e2" style="endArrow=block;html=1;strokeColor=#5f6368;" edge="1" parent="1" source="p19_s1" target="p19_o1">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_e3" style="endArrow=block;html=1;strokeColor=#5f6368;" edge="1" parent="1" source="p19_o1" target="p19_s2">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_e4" style="endArrow=block;html=1;strokeColor=#5f6368;" edge="1" parent="1" source="p19_s2" target="p19_o2">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_e5" style="endArrow=block;html=1;strokeColor=#5f6368;" edge="1" parent="1" source="p19_o2" target="p19_s3">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_e6" style="endArrow=block;html=1;strokeColor=#5f6368;" edge="1" parent="1" source="p19_o2" target="p19_s4">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_e7" style="endArrow=block;html=1;strokeColor=#5f6368;" edge="1" parent="1" source="p19_s3" target="p19_o3">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_e8" style="endArrow=block;html=1;strokeColor=#5f6368;" edge="1" parent="1" source="p19_s4" target="p19_o3">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_e9" style="endArrow=block;html=1;strokeColor=#5f6368;" edge="1" parent="1" source="p19_o3" target="p19_s5">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_e10" style="endArrow=block;html=1;strokeColor=#5f6368;" edge="1" parent="1" source="p19_s5" target="p19_o4">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p19_e11" style="endArrow=block;html=1;strokeColor=#5f6368;" edge="1" parent="1" source="p19_o4" target="p19_fb">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
  <diagram id="p20" name="20 · Advanced Concepts — Worked Examples">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1400" dy="900" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1720" pageHeight="1700" math="0" shadow="0">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
        <mxCell id="1" parent="0" />
        <mxCell id="p20_t" value="20 · ADVANCED CONCEPTS — WORKED EXAMPLES. Six advanced LLM-serving concepts most relevant to running finzorr in production, each explained in plain language with a concrete worked example — extends the ADVANCED CONCEPTS summary on page 13 and goes one angle further than the KV cache deep-dive on page 18." style="text;html=1;fontSize=15;fontStyle=1;align=left;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="12" width="1660" height="26" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p20_kv" value="KV CACHE — PREFIX CACHING (a new angle beyond page 18)&#xa;&#xa;Quick recap (full mechanic on page 18): within ONE response, a model caches the key/value math for tokens already generated, so each new token only attends against that cache instead of recomputing the whole sequence.&#xa;&#xa;NEW ANGLE — reuse ACROSS separate calls: if the very start of a prompt is byte-identical to a previous call, some providers can reuse that prefix&#39;s cached K/V instead of recomputing it from scratch, even though each call is a separate request.&#xa;&#xa;Worked example: every call finzorr&#39;s specialists send through app/infrastructure/llm/&#39;s OpenAICompatibleProvider to Groq starts with the SAME fixed guard/system instructions — say 400 tokens — followed by the user&#39;s actual question, say 30 tokens.&#xa;• Call 1: provider computes K/V for all 430 tokens.&#xa;• Call 2, moments later, same 400-token preamble: a provider that supports prefix caching recognizes the first 400 tokens are unchanged, reuses that cached K/V, and only computes K/V for the new 30-token question.&#xa;&#xa;Result: less repeated compute per call → often cheaper and faster time-to-first-token on repeat calls, with an IDENTICAL final output — the cached math is the same either way. Caveat: the shared prefix must be an exact match at the very start of the prompt; inserting a per-user variable before it breaks the reuse." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e8f0fe;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=6;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="60" width="530" height="460" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p20_moe" value="MIXTURE OF EXPERTS (MoE)&#xa;&#xa;Plain language: instead of running every part of the network for every token, a &quot;router&quot; picks just a few specialized sub-networks (&quot;experts&quot;) to actually use for each token, out of many available.&#xa;&#xa;Analogy: a large hospital has many specialist doctors, but a patient does not see EVERY doctor — a triage step routes them to the 1-2 specialists actually relevant to their case; MoE does something similar computationally.&#xa;&#xa;Worked example: say a MoE feed-forward layer has 8 experts total, and the router keeps the top 2 per token. For the token &quot;amortization&quot;, the router scores all 8 experts — expert 3 (finance/accounting patterns) scores 0.41, expert 7 (general morphology) scores 0.33, the rest score lower — so only experts 3 and 7 run their feed-forward math for this token; the other 6 sit idle. The very next token might route to a completely different pair. Net effect: the model can hold roughly 8x the total parameters of a same-sized dense model, while each token&#39;s actual compute cost stays close to a 2-expert-sized model.&#xa;&#xa;Tie to finzorr: the same SHAPE as app/orchestration/supervisor.py — a supervisor decides which specialist (nl2sql vs rag vs others) handles a request rather than running every specialist on every query. MoE applies that identical &quot;route to the relevant expert(s), skip the rest&quot; idea one level deeper, inside the model&#39;s own feed-forward layers, per TOKEN rather than per request." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=6;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="580" y="60" width="530" height="460" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p20_quant" value="QUANTIZATION&#xa;&#xa;Plain language: storing each model weight using fewer bits / less numeric precision than full precision.&#xa;&#xa;Worked example: a full-precision weight like 111.4271859 (typical fp16/fp32) can be stored as just 111 at low precision (int8-style) — some precision is lost, but it takes far less space to store and is faster to compute with. A weight like 0.0392156 might round to roughly 0.039. Applied across BILLIONS of weights — e.g. going from 16-bit down to 8-bit roughly halves the raw memory footprint — this is what lets large models run on far more modest hardware.&#xa;&#xa;Tie to finzorr: app/infrastructure/llm/&#39;s OpenAICompatibleProvider can call Ollama for a local model. Quantization is exactly what makes a local emergency-fallback model practical to run on a regular machine instead of requiring a data-center GPU — trading a small amount of precision for a large drop in memory and compute needs." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#f5f5f5;strokeColor=#999999;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=6;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1130" y="60" width="530" height="340" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p20_spec" value="SPECULATIVE DECODING&#xa;&#xa;Plain language: a small, fast &quot;draft&quot; model quickly guesses several tokens ahead, and the larger, slower model checks (verifies) that whole guessed batch in a single pass — rather than generating each token from scratch itself.&#xa;&#xa;Analogy: like a fast typist drafting a paragraph and an editor quickly confirming or correcting it in one pass, rather than the editor writing every single word themselves from scratch.&#xa;&#xa;Worked example: the draft model guesses the next 4 tokens are [&quot;the&quot;, &quot;portfolio&quot;, &quot;gained&quot;, &quot;3&quot;]. The large model then runs ONE verification pass over all 4 candidates together, not four separate generation steps:&#xa;• If it agrees with all 4 → all 4 are accepted at once, roughly 4 tokens for the cost of one large-model pass.&#xa;• If it disagrees at position 3 (it would have picked &quot;rose&quot;, not &quot;gained&quot;) → it accepts positions 1-2, corrects position 3, and drafting restarts from there.&#xa;&#xa;Either way, the FINAL output is identical to what the large model alone would have produced token-by-token — only the speed differs. When the draft model guesses well, this can be 2-3x faster." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e8f0fe;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=6;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="540" width="530" height="420" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p20_ctx" value="CONTEXT WINDOW COST — WHY IT GROWS FASTER THAN YOU&#39;D EXPECT&#xa;&#xa;Plain language: the computational cost of self-attention grows roughly with the SQUARE of how much text is in context — double the text, roughly 4x the attention cost, not 2x.&#xa;&#xa;Worked example: attending over 100 tokens might cost some baseline amount, roughly 100² = 10,000 &quot;units&quot; of pairwise attention computation (every token compares against every other token). Attending over 1,000 tokens (10x more text) costs roughly 1,000² = 1,000,000 units — 100x more compute, not just 10x.&#xa;&#xa;Tie to finzorr — this is exactly why RAG exists here: app/documents/ingest.py chunks and embeds uploaded documents (via nomic-embed-text) into app/infrastructure/vector_store.py (Qdrant); app/specialists/rag.py then retrieves only the relevant few chunks for a given question at query time, instead of stuffing an entire uploaded document into every prompt. Keeping the retrieved slice small keeps n small — which keeps the n² attention cost, and latency, manageable." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#fce8e6;strokeColor=#c5221f;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=6;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="580" y="540" width="530" height="360" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p20_cot" value="CHAIN OF THOUGHT&#xa;&#xa;Plain language: prompting (or training) a model to write out intermediate reasoning steps before its final answer, rather than jumping straight to a conclusion.&#xa;&#xa;Worked example — &quot;what is 15% of 240, then add 10&quot;:&#xa;• WITH chain of thought, the model might explicitly write: &quot;15% of 240 is 36, then 36 + 10 is 46&quot; before stating the final answer, 46.&#xa;• WITHOUT it, the model jumps straight to &quot;46&quot; with no visible reasoning — correct here, but more error-prone on longer multi-step problems, since a slip in an intermediate step has no visible checkpoint before it propagates straight into a wrong final answer.&#xa;&#xa;Tie to finzorr: app/specialists/nl2sql.py benefits from exactly this pattern — reasoning through &quot;which table, which filter, which aggregation&quot; as explicit steps before emitting a final SQL query is more reliable than generating the SQL in a single leap." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ede7f6;strokeColor=#7e57c2;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=6;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1130" y="540" width="530" height="380" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="p20_legend" value="LEGEND (standard color key used across this atlas)&#xa;&#xa;Teal = input / entry / user-facing&#xa;Blue = process / service / reference panel&#xa;Green = LIVE — actually built &amp; running in finzorr today&#xa;Amber (dashed) = PLANNED — identified gap, not yet built&#xa;Yellow = decision point / routing logic&#xa;Purple = output / synthesis / streamed result&#xa;Gray = dense reference / annotation panel&#xa;Red = risk / warning / incident&#xa;&#xa;On THIS page: blue marks provider- and model-serving mechanics (KV prefix caching, speculative decoding); yellow marks the MoE router as a decision point; gray marks the quantization reference panel; red flags the quadratic context-window cost as a genuine cost/latency risk; purple marks chain-of-thought since it shapes the model&#39;s final output. No teal/green/amber is used — this page is conceptual LLM-serving reference material, like page 13, not a build-status page. Concrete finzorr tie-ins (Groq calls via app/infrastructure/llm/, routing via app/orchestration/supervisor.py, RAG via app/documents/ingest.py, app/infrastructure/vector_store.py and app/specialists/rag.py, NL2SQL via app/specialists/nl2sql.py) are called out in text within each panel rather than color-coded." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#f5f5f5;strokeColor=#999999;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=6;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="980" width="1660" height="300" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>

        <mxCell id="p20_ex_t" value="EASY WORKED EXAMPLES — THE SAME NUMBERS AS EACH PANEL ABOVE, AS QUICK 3-STEP CHAINS. One tiny input→mechanism→output chain per concept, nothing new invented." style="text;html=1;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;align=left;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1330" width="1660" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>

